# Trivy gate baseline — pre-existing OS-package findings in the base images.
#
# ── What this file is NOT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# It is not a waiver, and it hides nothing. scripts/scan-image.sh runs Trivy
# twice and applies this file to ONE of the two passes:
#
#   report pass  --ignorefile /dev/null   every severity, every package type,
#                                         including everything listed here →
#                                         SARIF → GitHub code scanning.
#
#   gate pass    --ignorefile .trivyignore  fixable HIGH/CRITICAL OS packages,
#                                           minus this list → blocks the merge.
#
# The effect is that the gate blocks *regressions*. A newly disclosed CVE, or
# one a PR introduces via a base-image change, goes red on that PR. Debt that
# arrived with a third-party base image Atlas does not build stays visible in
# code scanning without pinning every unrelated PR red.
#
# ── Why there is a baseline at all ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There currently is not one. This file is deliberately EMPTY of entries, and
# that is the finished state rather than a gap — see below.
#
# It held 11 entries from 2026-07-27 (#4822) to 2026-08-12. Each was present in
# an upstream base image on the day the gate landed and none was fixable by
# bumping a pin: the pinned digest for oven/bun:1.3.13 IS the current digest for
# that tag, so upstream had simply not rebuilt with the patched packages.
# "Fixable" in Trivy's sense means the distro published a fixed package version
# — not that a fixed base image exists to move to.
#
# That original note named the fix and then declined it: "clearing them needs an
# apt/apk upgrade in the runner stages ... out of scope for the PR that added
# the gate." The upgrade is now in the runner stages, and it cleared all 11.
#
# ── Scope note ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Only OS packages belong here, because only OS packages are gated. Library
# findings inside the images (npm, Go modules) are reported to code scanning but
# never block.
#
# ⚠️ This paragraph used to end "— they are Dependabot's remit, and it can open
# the fix PR." That is false and was already corrected in scripts/scan-image.sh
# by #4878; the correction just never reached this file. Atlas uses bun, and
# `bun` is a separate Dependabot ecosystem from `npm` with NO security updates,
# so no configuration produces a fix PR here. Remediation for library findings
# is hand-authored root `overrides` plus parent bumps. Read the SCOPE section in
# scripts/scan-image.sh for the real detection -> visibility -> remediation
# handoff, and do not restate it here — one copy, one place to keep true.
#
# ── How to re-measure ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# After ANY change to a base-image pin or a runner-stage package install,
# re-scan and delete every entry that no longer appears. Scan the BUILT images,
# not just the bases: the runner stages now upgrade packages, so a base-image
# scan overstates what actually ships.
#
#   bash scripts/list-runtime-base-images.sh | while read -r i; do \
#     trivy image --scanners vuln --pkg-types os --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
#       --ignore-unfixed --ignorefile /dev/null "$i"; done
#
# Entries are global rather than per-image on purpose: the two runtime bases
# shared 7 of the original 11, so per-image files would duplicate and drift.
#
# ── The 11 entries that used to live here, and what replaced them ────────────
#
# Emptied 2026-08-12. All measurements below are from Trivy 0.72.0 — the pinned
# version — run against the actual images, not inferred from advisory text.
#
#   deploy/docs    caddy:2.10-alpine -> 2.11.4-alpine, digest-pinned, plus an
#                  `apk upgrade`. 128 unique CVEs -> 10; 6 CRITICAL -> 0;
#                  65 HIGH -> 6; gate-blocking 11 -> 0.
#
#   deploy/api     oven/bun:1.3.13 UNCHANGED (engines.bun holds it there — the
#   deploy/web     #2811 --isolate TLA regression), plus an `apt-get upgrade`.
#                  107 unique CVEs -> 75; 6 CRITICAL -> 4; 29 HIGH -> 18;
#                  gate-blocking 7 -> 0.
#
# ⚠️ The `apk upgrade` / `apt-get upgrade` is the load-bearing half, NOT the
# base-image bump. Two things this measured that reading advisories would not:
#
#   - `apk add --no-cache curl` is a SILENT NO-OP when the base already has
#     curl at a lower version. caddy:2.11.4-alpine ships curl 8.19.0-r0 and the
#     fix for CVE-2026-5773 / CVE-2026-6276 is 8.20.0-r0, which sits in the
#     v3.23/main repo the image is already pointed at. Without the explicit
#     upgrade the bump ADDS two gate-blocking CVEs instead of clearing them.
#
#   - Bumping oven/bun to 1.3.14 produces the IDENTICAL 75/4/0 as staying on
#     1.3.13 with the upgrade. The fixes are in the security repo, not the base
#     image, so there was never a security argument for moving that pin — which
#     matters, because moving it would have broken the isolated test runner.
#
# ── If a NEW entry is ever needed ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Prefer fixing the image. A baseline entry is correct only when the distro has
# published no fixed package (Trivy already filters those with --ignore-unfixed,
# so this is rarer than it looks) or when the fix is unreachable from the pinned
# repo snapshot. Always give it an `exp:` date — the expiry is the forcing
# function; once passed, Trivy stops honouring it and the gate goes red until it
# is fixed or consciously re-dated. Do not bulk-extend dates — re-scan first.
